/**
 * A stack that tracks the maximum element in O(1) time.
 * 
 * The clever bit: we don't store just the value—we store (value, count)
 * pairs. When the same maximum appears multiple times, we only pop it when
 * its count hits zero. This handles duplicates elegantly without auxiliary
 * space proportional to stack size.
 */
public class MaxTrackingStack {
    private static class Entry {
        int value;
        int count; // how many times this value is the current max

        Entry(int value, int count) {
            this.value = value;
            this.count = count;
        }
    }

    private final Deque<Entry> stack;

    public MaxTrackingStack() {
        this.stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    }

    /**
     * Push a value in O(1).
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        if (stack.isEmpty() || value > stack.peek().value) {
            // New maximum found
            stack.push(new Entry(value, 1));
        } else if (value == stack.peek().value) {
            // Same as current max, increment its count
            stack.peek().count++;
        }
        // If value < current max, we ignore it—max doesn't change
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return the top value in O(1).
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        Entry top = stack.peek();
        top.count--;
        if (top.count == 0) {
            stack.pop();
        }
        return top.value;
    }

    /**
     * Query the maximum element in O(1).
     */
    public int getMax() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.peek().value;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
}
```

**Why this design works:**

- **Push**: Compare incoming value to current max (peek). If larger or equal, push it; if smaller, ignore it. O(1).
- **Pop**: Decrement the count of whatever is logically on top. If count hits zero, physically remove from deque. O(1).
- **getMax**: Always at `stack.peek().value`. O(1).

**Example trace:**
```
push(5)     → stack: [(5,1)], max=5
push(3)     → stack: [(5,1)], max=5 (3 < 5, ignored)
push(5)     → stack: [(5,2)], max=5 (5 == 5, increment count)
pop()       → returns 5, stack: [(5,1)], max=5
pop()       → returns 5, stack: [], max=throws
```

The "surprise": instead of maintaining a separate auxiliary stack (the naive approach), we collapse equal maxima into a count, reducing space overhead for repetitive data while keeping all three operations strictly O(1) time and O(n) worst-case space.

