public class ConstantTimeProductStack {
    private static final class Node {
        int value;
        long product;  // product of all elements from bottom to this node
        Node prev;

        Node(int value, long product, Node prev) {
            this.value = value;
            this.product = product;
            this.prev = prev;
        }
    }

    private Node top = null;
    private int size = 0;

    /**
     * Push element onto stack. O(1).
     * Maintains cumulative product invariant.
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        long newProduct = (top == null) ? value : top.product * value;
        top = new Node(value, newProduct, top);
        size++;
    }

    /**
     * Pop element from stack. O(1).
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) throw new EmptyStackException();
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.prev;
        size--;
        return value;
    }

    /**
     * Query the product of all elements in stack. O(1).
     * Returns 1 for empty stack (multiplicative identity).
     */
    public long getProduct() {
        return (top == null) ? 1L : top.product;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

**Why this is interesting:**
- **Practical**: Useful for numerics pipelines, geometric computations, probability chains
- **Non-obvious**: Sum would be easier; product requires thinking about overflow (you get saturating semantics at long boundaries)
- **Pure composition**: Each node's product is deterministic from its lineage — no separate tracking needed

**Example usage:**
```java
ConstantTimeProductStack stack = new ConstantTimeProductStack();
stack.push(2);
stack.push(3);
stack.push(4);
System.out.println(stack.getProduct());  // 24
stack.pop();
System.out.println(stack.getProduct());  // 6
```

All three operations are **O(1) time, O(n) space** (n = stack depth).

