Here's an implementation with a **constant-time running product query** — useful for calculating geometric means or detecting zero elements in streams:

```java
public class ProductStack {
    private static final class Node {
        int value;
        long product;  // product of all elements up to and including this one
        Node next;

        Node(int value, long product, Node next) {
            this.value = value;
            this.product = product;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    private Node top;
    private int size;

    public ProductStack() {
        this.top = null;
        this.size = 0;
    }

    /**
     * Push a value onto the stack. O(1).
     */
    public void push(int value) {
        long newProduct;
        if (top == null) {
            newProduct = value;
        } else {
            // Compute product carefully to avoid overflow
            // For this example, cap at Long.MAX_VALUE
            long prev = top.product;
            if (prev > 0 && value > 0 && prev > Long.MAX_VALUE / value) {
                newProduct = Long.MAX_VALUE;
            } else if (prev < 0 && value < 0 && prev < Long.MIN_VALUE / value) {
                newProduct = Long.MIN_VALUE;
            } else {
                newProduct = prev * value;
            }
        }
        top = new Node(value, newProduct, top);
        size++;
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return the top value. O(1).
     */
    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return value;
    }

    /**
     * Return the running product of all elements in the stack. O(1).
     * (Useful for: checking if any zeros exist, computing geometric mean factors,
     *  or tracking sign parity across the entire stack)
     */
    public long getRunningProduct() {
        if (top == null) {
            return 1;  // Identity element for multiplication
        }
        return top.product;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

**Why this is interesting:**
- **Min/Max stacks** are common; running product is more unusual and practical
- **Zero detection**: If `getRunningProduct() == 0`, you know instantly a zero exists without scanning
- **Sign tracking**: The sign of the product tells you parity (odd or even count of negatives)
- **Geometric properties**: Useful in numerical algorithms needing product invariants

All three operations are truly O(1) with O(n) space overall.

