Here's a **MinTrackingStack** that supports push, pop, and **O(1) minimum queries**:

```java
public class MinTrackingStack<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private Node<T> top;
    private int size;

    private static class Node<T> {
        T value;
        T minAtOrBelow;  // tracks minimum from this node down to bottom
        Node<T> next;

        Node(T value, T minAtOrBelow, Node<T> next) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minAtOrBelow = minAtOrBelow;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    public MinTrackingStack() {
        this.top = null;
        this.size = 0;
    }

    /**
     * Push a value onto the stack in O(1).
     */
    public void push(T value) {
        T newMin = (top == null) 
            ? value 
            : value.compareTo(top.minAtOrBelow) < 0 ? value : top.minAtOrBelow;
        top = new Node<>(value, newMin, top);
        size++;
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return the top value in O(1).
     */
    public T pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        T value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return value;
    }

    /**
     * Return the minimum value currently in the stack in O(1).
     */
    public T getMin() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return top.minAtOrBelow;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

**Why this works:**

Each node caches the minimum of its entire substack below it. When you push, you compare the new value against the cached minimum of the previous top—O(1). When you pop, you just unlink—O(1). When you query `getMin()`, you read the cached value from the top node—O(1).

Space cost is O(n) with a small constant factor (one extra reference per node).

