Here's a **MinMaxStack** that tracks both minimum and maximum in O(1):

```java
public class MinMaxStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int min;
        int max;

        Node(int value, int min, int max) {
            this.value = value;
            this.min = min;
            this.max = max;
        }
    }

    private List<Node> stack;

    public MinMaxStack() {
        stack = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        int newMin = stack.isEmpty() ? value : Math.min(value, stack.get(stack.size() - 1).min);
        int newMax = stack.isEmpty() ? value : Math.max(value, stack.get(stack.size() - 1).max);
        stack.add(new Node(value, newMin, newMax));
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.remove(stack.size() - 1).value;
    }

    public int getMin() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.get(stack.size() - 1).min;
    }

    public int getMax() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.get(stack.size() - 1).max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinMaxStack s = new MinMaxStack();
        s.push(5);
        s.push(2);
        s.push(8);
        s.push(1);

        System.out.println("Min: " + s.getMin());  // 1
        System.out.println("Max: " + s.getMax());  // 8
        s.pop();
        System.out.println("Min: " + s.getMin());  // 2
        System.out.println("Max: " + s.getMax());  // 8
    }
}
```

**Key idea:** Each node caches the min and max of *all elements below it*. When you push, compute the new min/max by comparing the incoming value against the previous top's cached bounds. When you pop, you discard that state — the next top already has the correct min/max precomputed. No auxiliary data structures needed.
